# 1Password env-file template — `op run --env-file=.env.op -- terraform <cmd>`
# resolves the op:// references to real secret values and injects them as env
# vars, so they never touch shell scrollback, history, or committed files.
#
# This file is committed (it holds op:// REFERENCES, not secret values). Anyone
# with read access to the referenced 1Password items gets the values; anyone
# without gets `op` errors and nothing leaks. Mirrors the AgenticOS
# state-backend/.env.op pattern.
#
# GOL-264 blocker (CEO Rick): the storefront CF token must be stored in
# 1Password with these permissions — Zone:Read, Zone WAF:Edit,
# Firewall Services:Edit, Cache Rules:Edit — under the Grove admin vault. Item
# name below is provisional; align with GOL-88 secrets-pipeline naming when the
# token lands.
#
# State backend creds: the Grove `grove-tf-state` Spaces bucket key, surfaced as
# AWS_* the S3 backend consumes (same convention as agenticos-tfstate).

TF_VAR_cloudflare_api_token=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Cloudflare/storefront_api_token
TF_VAR_monitoring_secret_header_value=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Cloudflare/monitoring_shared_secret

# Remote state (grove-tf-state bucket-scoped Spaces key):
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_tfstate_access_key_id
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_tfstate_secret_key
